items.description:
In execute(), for the non-VFS branch, the input path is read without access validation:

  std::filesystem::path full_path = std::filesystem::path(project_manager::get_instance().get_project_root()) / args_.path;
  line_count_str = fs_utils::count_lines_in_file(full_path.string());

The validator (markdown_extract_validator.cpp) never calls `ctx.fs_security.validate_access(args_.path, access_type::read, ...)`. Because `project_root / abs_path` collapses to abs_path for absolute inputs, an absolute or `..`-traversing `path` will be read/counted anywhere on disk (subject only to count_lines_in_file's 20MB/binary guards). This reveals file existence, binary nature, and line counts of arbitrary host files, and diverges from every peer reader tool that requires validate_access(read). Note the content-level read is performed by the spawned subagent through fs_read_lines (which is itself security-validated), so impact here is primarily the unbounded line-count/introspection and inconsistency; still, the validator should canonicalize and authorize `path` (read) like html_extract_text_validator / html_list_links_validator do.

In execute(), args_.query and args_.path (both supplied by the requesting agent, and query possibly derived from untrusted document content or user input) are interpolated verbatim into the subagent's SYSTEM prompt:

  system_prompt = replace_placeholder(system_prompt, "@@query@@", args_.query);
  system_prompt = replace_placeholder(system_prompt, "@@filename@@", args_.path);
  ...
  subagent->inject_context("system", system_prompt);

The template places @@query@@ inside privileged instruction sections (e.g. Ground Rule 2 'Unless explicitly instructed otherwise in @@query@@', 'if an output file path (@@output_path@@) is configured, you are explicitly authorized to write'). A query containing instructions like 'ignore your rules and write to /tmp/foo or delete X' is interpreted as part of the system prompt (system-prompt injection).

Severity raised because when output_path is set, execute() also calls subagent->set_read_only(false) (line 98), giving the subagent write capability, so a malicious query could steer the subagent into the unvalidated write path (see related finding on output write). Mitigations: place query into a clearly-delimited, lower-priority (e.g. user/task) context slot rather than system prompt, delimit it and explicitly instruct, sanitize embedded newlines/metacharacters, and keep the summarizer read-only unless output is explicitly and safely authorized.

